	"c-systems-engineering-cse0-setup-and-tooling-supplemental-c-systems-engineering-setup-transfer-practice":
		'### Transfer Practice: Compare compiler results at classification boundaries\n\nTransfer the first clean build to independent boundary evidence. Compiler choice can change diagnostics and object details while the documented function results remain the same.\n\n## Starting point and saved work\n\nUse the maintained C Systems Engineering as the starting point. This card opens that project\'s saved learner program while retaining its own lesson progress and return link. Complete any unfinished tasks before the changed cases below. Save the learner work before opening the instructor reference; it implements the API used by these examples.\n\n## Changed case and expected results\n\nPredict classify_reading for INT_MIN, -1, 0, 1, 99, 100, 101 and INT_MAX. Negative values are below range; zero through one hundred are within range; larger values are above range. Run the same case with Clang and GCC, record versions and require identical results. The case uses reading.h and the completed supplied implementation. Compile main.c to an object with its demonstration entry point renamed, then link the separate case driver; changing the main macro in both files would remove the case entry point.\n\n## Executable case\n\nAdd case.c inside the selected role directory. Compile it separately from the supplied main and test drivers; the library file choices below are intentional. The CHECK macro stays active in Release builds and exits unsuccessfully when a result differs.\n\n~~~c\n#include <stdio.h>\n#include <stdint.h>\n#include <stddef.h>\n#include <limits.h>\n#include <string.h>\n#define CHECK(c)                                                               \\\n  do {                                                                         \\\n    if (!(c)) {                                                                \\\n      fprintf(stderr, "check failed at line %d\\n", __LINE__);                  \\\n      return 1;                                                                \\\n    }                                                                          \\\n  } while (0)\n#include "reading.h"\n\nint main(void) {\n  const int inputs[] = {INT_MIN, -1, 0, 1, 99, 100, 101, INT_MAX};\n  const int expected[] = {-1, -1, 0, 0, 0, 0, 1, 1};\n  for (size_t i = 0; i < sizeof inputs / sizeof inputs[0]; ++i)\n    CHECK(classify_reading(inputs[i]) == expected[i]);\n  puts("Compare compiler results at classification boundaries: accepted");\n  return 0;\n}\n~~~\n\nFrom that role directory:\n\n~~~sh\nclang -std=c17 -Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic -Wconversion -Werror -I. -Dmain=cse_demo_main -c main.c -o demo.o\nclang -std=c17 -Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic -Wconversion -Werror -I. demo.o case.c -o case\n./case\n~~~\n\nUse gcc in place of clang for GCC. A successful run prints:\n\n~~~text\nCompare compiler results at classification boundaries: accepted\n~~~\n\n## Additional evidence\n\nAdd two values of opposite signs with the same magnitude and explain why zero belongs to the accepted interval. Keep strict warnings enabled for both compilers. Record a build, contract-test and case-driver result separately.\n\n## Explanation and guided review\n\nExplain which results come from the interface and which observations depend on a compiler or run. An identical healthy output does not replace the required failure and diagnostic checks in the opening guide. Record predictions, actual results and one corrected discrepancy. A guided walkthrough uses the same artifacts and asks for the prediction before execution.\n\n## Complete base-project contract and build guide\n\nThis required project establishes a repeatable build, test, debugger, and\nsanitizer workflow. The small reading classifier keeps the algorithm visible:\nvalues below 0 map to -1, values from 0 through 100 map to 0, and values above\n100 map to 1. No arithmetic is needed, including at INT_MIN and INT_MAX.\n\n## Read the source before building\n\n`reading.h` declares the contract. `main.c` defines the function and prints a\nfive-reading trace. `tests.c` checks nine independent fixtures. In the learner\nfolder only the body of `classify_reading` needs completion; preserve the public\nsignature, driver, and tests. The reference implements the same contract.\nAn untouched learner builds, then exits 78 with an unfinished-work message.\nIts contract test fails. A successful compiler invocation does not establish\ncorrect behavior.\n\nFor value 100 the first comparison is false and the second comparison is also\nfalse, so the result is 0. Changing `> 100` to `>= 100` is a controlled\nregression: the value-100 fixture must reject it.\n\n## Native setup and first run\n\nUse a Clang or GCC C17 compiler, CMake 3.20 or newer, and LLDB or GDB. On macOS,\nApple command-line tools supply Clang and LLDB. A Unix-style terminal is also\navailable in Windows through WSL2. Record each installed tool\'s version.\nThe site editor can store source; native compilation and diagnostics require\nthe local toolchain described here.\n\nFrom the learner folder:\n\n```sh\ncmake -S . -B build -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug\ncmake --build build\nctest --test-dir build --output-on-failure\n./build/cse0_starter\n```\n\nThe last two commands initially fail as described above. Complete the named\nfunction and repeat both commands. From the reference folder the corresponding\ncommands are:\n\n```sh\ncmake -S . -B build -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug\ncmake --build build\nctest --test-dir build --output-on-failure\n./build/cse0_solution\n```\n\nBoth roles enforce C17 and `-Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic -Werror`. The complete trace\nis:\n\n```text\n-1 => -1\n0 => 0\n50 => 0\n100 => 0\n101 => 1\n```\n\nThe tests cover INT_MIN, -1, 0, 1, 50, 99, 100, 101, and INT_MAX. Explain why\nthose fixtures exercise both boundaries and all three categories. Predict one\nadditional case, add it without removing existing fixtures, and check it.\n\n## Diagnostic and debugger pass\n\nBuild the completed role with address and undefined-behavior diagnostics:\n\n```sh\ncmake -S . -B build-sanitized -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug -DCSE_SANITIZERS=ON\ncmake --build build-sanitized\nctest --test-dir build-sanitized --output-on-failure\n```\n\n`unsafe_probe.c` is a separate, deliberately invalid fixture outside normal\nbuilds. From `CSE-05-cse0-setup-and-tooling` (one level above either role), compile\nand run it in isolation:\n\n```sh\nclang -std=c17 -g -O0 -fsanitize=address,undefined -fno-omit-frame-pointer unsafe_probe.c -o build-unsafe-probe\n./build-unsafe-probe\n```\n\nIt indexes element 4 in a four-element array. The diagnostic must reject the\nrun and identify the invalid access. Do not treat its failure as a successful\napplication run. The corrected classifier remains a separate clean program.\n\nOpen the completed executable in LLDB or GDB, set a breakpoint in\n`classify_reading`, run it, inspect `value`, and step through the comparisons.\nRecord one observation connecting source, state, and the printed result.\n\n## Completion and walkthrough\n\nSubmit tool versions, clean build/test output, the five-line trace, a diagnosed\nunsafe fixture, a clean sanitized classifier run, and a test that rejects the\ncontrolled boundary regression. This sequence works for independent study;\nwith an instructor, compare predictions before each run and use diagnostic\noutput to explain the correction. The notebook is evidence, not another\nimplementation of the same helper. Reference comparison follows a saved\nlearner attempt.',
