		'### Transfer Practice: Omit a translation unit and diagnose the missing definition\n\nTransfer the source/object/link distinction to an intentional missing-definition case. A header declaration allows a call to compile but does not supply the function body.\n\n## Starting point and saved work\n\nUse the maintained Why C for Systems Work as the starting point. This card opens that project\'s saved learner program while retaining its own lesson progress and return link. Complete any unfinished tasks before the changed cases below. Save the learner work before opening the instructor reference; it implements the API used by these examples.\n\n## Changed case and expected results\n\nCompile main.c and reading.c without labels.c and attempt to link them. The missing reading_label definition must reject the link. Add the unchanged labels.c object and require a healthy executable. Then link the separate case below with both library files and verify all category labels. Keep the supplied header declarations in place; removing a declaration would turn this into a different compile-time problem.\n\n## Executable case\n\nAdd case.c inside the selected role directory. Compile it separately from the supplied main and test drivers; the library file choices below are intentional. The CHECK macro stays active in Release builds and exits unsuccessfully when a result differs.\n\n~~~c\n#include <stdio.h>\n#include <stdint.h>\n#include <stddef.h>\n#include <limits.h>\n#include <string.h>\n#define CHECK(c)                                                               \\\n  do {                                                                         \\\n    if (!(c)) {                                                                \\\n      fprintf(stderr, "check failed at line %d\\n", __LINE__);                  \\\n      return 1;                                                                \\\n    }                                                                          \\\n  } while (0)\n#include "reading.h"\n\nint main(void) {\n  CHECK(classify_reading(-1) == -1);\n  CHECK(classify_reading(100) == 0);\n  CHECK(classify_reading(101) == 1);\n  CHECK(strcmp(reading_label(-1), "below") == 0);\n  CHECK(strcmp(reading_label(0), "within") == 0);\n  CHECK(strcmp(reading_label(1), "above") == 0);\n  CHECK(strcmp(reading_label(2), "invalid") == 0);\n  puts("Omit a translation unit and diagnose the missing definition: accepted");\n  return 0;\n}\n~~~\n\nFrom that role directory:\n\n~~~sh\nclang -std=c17 -Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic -Wconversion -Werror -I. reading.c labels.c case.c -o case\n./case\n~~~\n\nUse gcc in place of clang for GCC. A successful run prints:\n\n~~~text\nOmit a translation unit and diagnose the missing definition: accepted\n~~~\n\n## Separate link experiment\n\nCompile first, then link without labels.c:\n\n~~~sh\nclang -std=c17 -Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic -Werror -c main.c reading.c\nclang main.o reading.o -o missing-label\n~~~\n\nObject compilation succeeds and the link rejects the missing reading_label definition. Restore the complete file set:\n\n~~~sh\nclang -std=c17 -Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic -Werror -c labels.c\nclang main.o reading.o labels.o -o complete-demo\n./complete-demo\n~~~\n\n## Additional evidence\n\nRecord the successful object compilation and failed link as separate stages. Restore the full file set and rerun its contracts. Identify one declaration, one definition and one call site for reading_label.\n\n## Explanation and guided review\n\nExplain why an include guard prevents repeated header processing within a translation unit but does not resolve a missing definition at link time. Record predictions, actual results and one corrected discrepancy. A guided walkthrough uses the same artifacts and asks for the prediction before execution.\n\n## Complete base-project contract and build guide\n\nThis required project deliberately reuses the completed setup classifier\'s\nbehavior. The new goal is its build structure: separate translation units,\nheader declarations, object files, and the linker. Reimplementing the previous\nalgorithm is not the requirement.\n\n## Files and contract\n\n- `reading.h`: declarations shared by all source files; its include guard\n  prevents repeated declarations within one translation unit.\n- `reading.c`: the provided classifier from setup.\n- `labels.c`: the learner task. Implement `reading_label`: categories -1, 0,\n  and 1 produce `below`, `within`, and `above`; every other category produces\n  `invalid`. Return pointers to string literals; callers do not free them.\n- `main.c`: the unchanged driver connecting both functions.\n- `tests.c`: six label fixtures plus four cases spanning both source files.\n\nThe starter builds but its label helper returns NULL, so its driver exits 78\nand its tests fail safely. Complete only that helper. Compare with the separate\nreference after saving an attempt. Both folders build independently.\n\n## Clean build and expected trace\n\nFrom the learner folder:\n\n```sh\ncmake -S . -B build -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug\ncmake --build build\nctest --test-dir build --output-on-failure\n./build/cse1_link_starter\n```\n\nBefore completion the test and program commands fail. From the reference folder:\n\n```sh\ncmake -S . -B build -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug\ncmake --build build\nctest --test-dir build --output-on-failure\n./build/cse1_link_solution\n```\n\nThe complete trace is:\n\n```text\n-1 => below\n0 => within\n50 => within\n100 => within\n101 => above\n```\n\nFor value 101, `reading.c` returns category 1. `main.c` passes that category to\n`labels.c`, obtains the literal `above`, and prints it. The declaration lets\n`main.c` compile before the linker connects those definitions.\n\n## Observe the source-to-program boundary\n\nRun these commands from a completed role. Preprocessed source includes the\nheader text; assembly represents generated instructions; an object file has\ncompiled code plus symbol information but has not been linked into this\nprogram.\n\n```sh\ncc -std=c17 -E main.c -o main.i\ncc -std=c17 -S main.c -o main.s\ncc -std=c17 -Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic -Werror -c main.c reading.c labels.c\nnm main.o reading.o labels.o\ncc main.o reading.o labels.o -o linked-reading\n./linked-reading\n```\n\nInspect the undefined references in `main.o` and the definitions in the other\nobjects. Symbol decoration varies by platform, so explain the relationship\nrather than copying an exact `nm` spelling.\n\nThese two controlled link commands must fail:\n\n```sh\ncc main.o reading.o -o missing-label-definition\ncc main.o reading.o labels.o labels.o -o duplicate-label-definition\n```\n\nThe first omits the object defining `reading_label`. The second supplies that\ndefinition twice. Compile each source once and use the successful link command\nto recover. Header guards act during preprocessing; they do not resolve\nmultiple external definitions in different translation units.\n\n## Check and explain\n\nThe label tests cover -1, 0, 1, INT_MIN, 2, and INT_MAX. The cross-file tests\ncover readings -1, 0, 100, and 101. Add a new outside-range category and one new\nwithin-range reading after predicting their results. Preserve existing tests.\nUse `-DCSE_SANITIZERS=ON` in a separate CMake build for a clean diagnostic pass.\n\nCompletion includes the clean trace, passing fixtures, one preprocessed or\nassembly observation, symbol evidence, both expected link failures, and a\nsuccessful clean rebuild. For an instructor walkthrough, predict each failure\nbefore running it; independently, write the prediction first and compare the\nactual message afterward. Source editing and native build evidence are\nseparate parts of the workflow.',
